# Draft Capture (`>draft`)

`>draft: <notes>` captures raw notes as a draft analysis. It is the
**raw-capture rung** of the analysis ladder: it never asks a question, and it
files the note on the right initiative when a draft for that initiative already
exists. The eliciting alternatives are `>scope` (batched questions) and
`>analysis` (authoring or extending a full analysis); all three converge on a
`status: draft` analysis.

## Invocation

- Trigger: `>draft`.
- Payload: everything after the first `:` on the same line, taken **verbatim** to
  the end of the turn — this is the note; multi-line payload is allowed. No
  flags, no quoting. With no `:`, the whole message is the note.
- The invocation **is** the approval to write exactly one capture (one create, or
  one append). The file- and plan-creation gates are untouched.

## Capture ritual

1. **Resolve the objective.** Compare the note with the existing `status: draft`
   analyses (`sdt context list --type analysis`, then read their `objective`,
   `title` and `topics`). Trust a match only when it is unambiguous.
2. **Append or create.**
   - If **exactly one** existing `status: draft` analysis matches the objective,
     append the note to its body under a `## Notes` section (create the heading
     when absent) and refresh `updated` with `sdt context touch`.
   - If **none or more than one** matches, create a new draft:
     `sdt context new --type analysis --title "<derived title>" --status draft
     --objective <slug> --input "<note>"`. Omit `--objective` when nothing
     resolves. Never block the capture on a question; never guess a merge.
3. **Derive a title and summary** from the note (first meaningful line as the
   title; one or two sentences as `summary`). Keep the note itself verbatim in
   the body.
4. **Report** the file the capture touched (path, created vs appended).

## Rules

- Never ask a question mid-capture: resolve, write, report. A note that matches
  nothing is still a valid draft with no objective.
- One invocation writes one file mutation — one create, or one append.
- The capture is a plain `context/analysis/` document in `draft` state; no new
  kind, no new directory, no side file.
- Draining a draft is normal: `>scope` elicits and fills the analysis, `>plan`
  turns it into a plan. The draft is not migrated.
- No H1 in the created body (document convention); write `## ` sections.
